---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { homeHref } from '../lib/posts';
---

<Layout title="Skills">
  <Header />
  <article class="skills-page">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Skills</h1>

      <section id="programming" class="skill-depth">
        <h2>Programming &amp; Data</h2>
        <p>Python (pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, Beautiful Soup), R (tidyverse, ggplot2, Shiny), SQL, Excel, Jupyter.</p>
        <p>At <a href={homeHref('experience#moth')}>The Moth</a>, a first pass in R (tidyverse, ggplot2) caught NaNs and showed trends. A later pandas script, about 20 lines, caught what R missed, dropped duplicates and missing values, and wrote clean files. It was self-taught with research and generative AI.</p>
        <p>I use SQL for SELECT and GROUP BY, from GEOG 377, plus some queries on the Moth data. I learned Shiny in STAT 436. I'm currently learning machine learning in STAT 451 (Spring 2027).</p>
      </section>

      <section id="gis" class="skill-depth">
        <h2>GIS &amp; Cartography</h2>
        <p>ArcGIS Pro, spatial joins, choropleth mapping, data classification, Adobe Illustrator.</p>
        <p>For <a href={homeHref('experience#schools')}>Wisconsin Public School Spending Analysis</a>, spatial joins in ArcGIS Pro matched spending and graduation data to district boundaries, and a bivariate choropleth compared spending per student with graduation rate. The final layout was designed in ArcGIS Pro and Illustrator.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="tools" class="skill-depth">
        <h2>Tools &amp; Communication</h2>
        <p>Git/GitHub, RStudio, regression analysis, technical documentation, data storytelling, data visualization, bootstrapping.</p>
        <p>At <a href={homeHref('experience#doit')}>UW–Madison DoIT</a> I triage tickets in WiscIT, document troubleshooting steps, and coach students and staff on phishing and MFA. Moth reports give each chart one question, a plain-words takeaway, and a recommendation. The <a href={homeHref('experience#crypto')}>crypto project</a> used a Fisher z-test and a bootstrap.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="coursework" class="coursework-section">
        <div class="section-header">
          <h2>Coursework</h2>
        </div>
        <dl class="coursework">
          <div>
            <dt>Data Science &amp; Statistics</dt>
            <dd>Data Science Modeling I &amp; II, Data Science Programming I &amp; II, Statistical Data Visualization, Intro to Probability &amp; Mathematical Statistics I (in progress)</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>GIS &amp; Cartography</dt>
            <dd>Intro to Cartography, Intro to Geographic Information Systems (in progress)</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Math, CS &amp; Engineering</dt>
            <dd>Calculus I–III, Linear Algebra, Programming I, General Physics, Intro to Electrical Engineering, Intro to Computer Engineering</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Spring 2027</dt>
            <dd>Machine Learning, Data Science with R, Intro to AI &amp; Data Ethics</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
